<p>Serbian President Aleksandar Vučić said authorities found detonators and explosives &quot;of devastating power&quot; near the Balkan Stream/TurkStream pipeline close to the Serbian village of Velebit, near the Hungarian border. Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán said Serbia informed him of the discovery on Sunday. The pipeline carries Russian natural gas from Turkey through the Balkans into Serbia and on to Hungary, so the find matters for regional energy security and is especially politically sensitive just one week before Hungary’s election. The discovery quickly set off competing claims about who might be responsible. Orbán appeared to suggest Ukrainian involvement, while Hungarian Foreign Minister Péter Szijjártó said he was not directly accusing Ukraine but did not rule it out. Ukraine’s Foreign Ministry denied any role and said the accusation was part of a Russian false-flag operation tied to broader interference in Hungary’s election, while Hungary’s opposition candidate raised the possibility that the incident itself was staged to influence voters.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/BUbVr1q3Tpf2tYyO_co8OTR5R2i72DxtPzaXjxEntbGTidNWFQLQMGhp6yrL98daWbcmLgMg3UPRPLpWy47vZ2b5roW2QDq_8XCCNqlkhqUrCVY6206HD9bCwdd7JWZO_A5swxAGNd_MnF4iowZWvQlD4XgiAP5K33maWQ-Utj5O4NPnz7CEeK20AD8OnASHhC9j74V6cbZdBIwvL3TaipGoL0vCilG6tCXnw0nq18epJhh6-ff00o3NJQDEE6yXmhoj_q5Z1ue39bcRkifSp7Kkrw' alt='Serbian President Aleksandar Vučić, one of the leaders who disclosed the pipeline explosives find.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Poll pressure: The episode landed in the final week of Hungary’s campaign. The Guardian reported Orbán was trailing in polls, and the BBC highlighted fears that the story could sway voters.</li><li>Border sensitivity: The reported find was near Velebit, close to the Hungarian border, so a security incident on Serbian territory immediately raised questions about Hungary’s gas imports and domestic politics.</li><li>Competing &quot;false flag&quot; narratives: Kyiv said Moscow was manufacturing the accusation as part of election interference, while Hungary’s opposition said the incident itself may have been staged to shape the vote.</li><li>Attribution vacuum: The cited reports named no confirmed perpetrator, leaving political messaging from Budapest, Kyiv, and Hungary’s opposition to drive the early public narrative.</li></ul><blockquote>Explosives of devastating force - Aleksandar Vučić</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Viktor Orbán: Orbán said Serbia had informed Hungary about the explosives find and appeared to suggest Ukraine may have been involved in planning sabotage against the pipeline. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3349104/orban-appears-blame-kyiv-serbian-gas-pipeline-explosives-find'>SCMP</a>)</li><li>Ukrainian Foreign Ministry: Spokesperson Heorhii Tykhyi rejected any Ukrainian role and described the allegation as part of a Russian false-flag operation linked to Moscow’s interference in Hungary’s election. (<a href='https://meduza.io/en/news/2026/04/06/hungary-hints-at-ukraine-s-role-in-serbia-pipeline-explosives-find-kyiv-denies'>Meduza</a>)</li><li>Péter Magyar: Hungary’s main opposition figure said the incident may have been staged to influence voters ahead of the election. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/05/viktor-orban-hungary-election-serbia-explosives-gas-pipeline'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Péter Szijjártó: Hungary’s foreign minister did not directly accuse Ukraine but said he could not rule out possible Ukrainian involvement. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/explosives-found-near-serbian-pipeline-that-carries-russian-gas-say-leaders/article70827455.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/05/viktor-orban-hungary-election-serbia-explosives-gas-pipeline'>Hungarian PM faces ‘false flag’ claims after Serbia says explosives found near pipeline</a> - theguardian.c